{"chunk_id": "29f035e5ece4", "text": "This passage recounts the miraculous revival of Abhimanyu\u2019s stillborn son. Uttara, overwhelmed with grief, laments over the lifeless child while the royal women gather around her. Krishna, moved by her sorrow, withdraws the destructive Brahma-weapon and vows\u2014by the truthfulness of his life, his devotion to righteousness, and his victories over evil\u2014that the child will live. His words manifest power: the infant stirs and returns to life, bringing immense relief and celebration to the Kuru household. The child is later named Parikshit because he is born when the Kuru line is nearly extinguished after the great war. The episode underscores Krishna\u2019s divine authority, the sanctity of truth, and the fragile survival of the Kuru dynasty through this single heir.\nKrishna revives Abhimanyu\u2019s stillborn son, names him Parikshit, and the Pandavas return to a joyfully decorated Hastinapura.\nThemes: divine intervention, revival and rebirth, dynastic survival, royal celebration", "vector": [0.0016652796184644103, -0.016759144142270088, -0.011161508969962597, -0.0014542710268869996, -0.012120332568883896, 0.011458609253168106, -0.00647543091326952, -0.002557423897087574, -0.014206784777343273, -0.009527458809316158, 0.017880022525787354, 0.003784649772569537, 0.004605051130056381, -0.004895398858934641, -0.011863745748996735, -0.002964248415082693, 0.04688779264688492, -0.015611258335411549, 0.02713063731789589, -0.02051340974867344, 0.014746966771781445, -0.0019176459172740579, -0.006127689033746719, 0.013322236947715282, -0.024213654920458794, 0.02278217300772667, 0.011168261989951134, -0.02691456489264965, 0.02833254262804985, -0.028062451630830765, -0.019054917618632317, -0.01389618031680584, -0.007245190441608429, -0.016029899939894676, -0.02079700492322445, -0.005395066924393177, -0.0026401393115520477, -0.014760471880435944, 0.00907505676150322, -0.005759689956903458, -0.0006887319614179432, -0.004713087342679